Rating guidelines: A company that completely abstains from right-wing political spending and dark money, bans revolving-door lobbying, and actively champions campaign finance reform and transparency (such as opposing Citizens United) gets a 100. A company that engages only in standard, fully disclosed political spending and lobbying strictly to protect baseline operational interests gets a 50. A company that channels dark money through 501(c)(4)s, right-wing think tanks, or Super PACs, weaponizes revolving-door lobbying, or funds lawmakers whose agendas directly undermine the company's stated ESG values gets a 1.